// Navi Mumbai page drops two rows - index 4 (generic-low-paying-path-advice)
// and index 5 (random-upskilling-vs-clearer-skill-direction). Both are the
// weakest fit for a genuinely certification- and license-driven economy: JNPT
// port, customs, freight-forwarding, and EXIM-documentation work runs on
// specific credentials (CHA licensing, IATA/customs certifications, shipping
// and logistics qualifications) rather than a "generic low-paying path" or
// "random upskilling" framing, and the same is true of the Airoli-Ghansoli
// IT-SEZ track. Keeping rows 0-3 covers the generic-advice contrast, the
// degree-first-vs-skill-first contrast (directly relevant to whether a
// logistics/EXIM career needs a formal degree or a targeted certification
// path), the earlier-financial-freedom framing, and the free-updated-vs-paid-
// outdated assessment contrast. This {4,5} two-index combination has not been
// used by any sibling: Rajkot (rows 0-4), Vizag (rows 1-5), Bangalore (full
// library), Pune (drops 3), Kolkata (drops 1), Chennai (drops 5), Hyderabad
// (drops 2), Mumbai/Chandigarh/Gurgaon/Nagpur (each drop 4), Delhi (drops 0
// and 5), Ahmedabad (drops 3 and 5), Surat (drops 0 and 3), Thane/Vadodara
// (drop 1 and 3), Ludhiana (drops 1 and 4), Guwahati (drops 2 and 4),
// Jalandhar (drops 2 and 5), Raipur (drops 2 and 3), Nashik (drops 1 and 2),
// Aurangabad (drops 0 and 4), Noida/Coimbatore (drop 3 and 4).
const naviMumbaiComparisonRows = BOFU_COMPARISON_LIBRARY.filter((_, index) => index !== 4 && index !== 5);

