    title: "JNPT anchors a real, large logistics and EXIM career economy that no other city sibling has",
    body: "Jawaharlal Nehru Port, at Nhava Sheva on Navi Mumbai's edge, is India's largest container port, handling a large share of the country's total containerised cargo. That single fact builds an entire career economy around it: customs house agents and customs brokerage, freight forwarding and cargo consolidation, container terminal operations across multiple private terminal operators, shipping-line and vessel-operations roles, marine and port engineering, cargo surveying and inspection, and a wide EXIM (export-import) documentation and trade-compliance layer that a purely finance-or-tech city like Mumbai or an office-corridor city like Thane simply does not offer at this scale.",
  },
  {
    title: 'This is a certification- and license-driven economy, not a pure-degree one',
    body: "Much of the port and logistics workforce builds a career through targeted certifications and licenses rather than a single fixed degree path - a customs house agent license, IATA and freight-forwarding certifications, supply-chain and logistics diplomas, and specialised EXIM-documentation training. For someone deciding what to study or upskill toward, that changes the honest calculation: a high-value skill portfolio built around the right certification can open genuinely well-paying port and trade roles faster than chasing a generic degree with no logistics specialisation attached to it.",
  },
  {
    title: "A dedicated logistics-park and warehousing layer has grown up around the port itself",
    body: "Bonded warehousing, container freight stations, logistics parks, and third-party logistics (3PL) operators have built up around JNPT and the wider Uran-Nhava Sheva belt to support the port's throughput, adding supply-chain planning, warehouse management, and inventory and distribution roles on top of the port-operations layer itself. Achieving earlier financial freedom from this specific economy usually means picking a lane inside this chain - port operations, customs and compliance, freight and forwarding, or warehousing and supply chain - rather than treating 'logistics' as one interchangeable label.",
  },
];

const cidcoIdentityItems = [
  {
    title: "Navi Mumbai was deliberately planned by CIDCO, not grown organically like Mumbai or Thane",
    body: "Navi Mumbai exists because the City and Industrial Development Corporation of Maharashtra (CIDCO) planned it from the 1970s onward as a satellite city built specifically to relieve pressure on an overcrowded Mumbai - a genuinely different civic backstory from Mumbai's centuries-old organic growth or Thane's older mill-and-lake-town history. The city is laid out in numbered planned nodes - Vashi, Nerul, CBD Belapur, Kharghar, Kopar Khairane, and others - each built around its own civic centre, market, and residential sectors, administered today by the Navi Mumbai Municipal Corporation (NMMC) alongside CIDCO's continuing role in newer nodes.",
  },
  {
    title: 'That planned-city identity makes Navi Mumbai a younger, more resettled population than Thane\'s multi-generational base',
    body: "Because the city itself is a relatively recent, deliberately built satellite rather than an old town families have lived in for generations, a meaningfully larger share of Navi Mumbai's population moved here as adults or as part of a resettled or relocated household, rather than being born into a multi-generational local identity the way a large share of Thane's population is. That changes the shape of a career conversation here: family expectation is often shaped by where a household moved from, not by decades of local precedent in Navi Mumbai itself, and a good counselling conversation should recognise that instead of assuming Thane's settled, multi-generational framing applies here too.",
  },
  {
    title: "A genuine wholesale-trade economy sits inside the planned-city layout: Asia's largest agricultural produce market is here",
    body: "Vashi's Agricultural Produce Market Committee (APMC) market is one of the largest wholesale markets of its kind in Asia, handling grains, vegetables, fruit, and other produce trade at a scale that supports its own real economy of trading, logistics, cold-chain, and wholesale-commerce careers - a genuinely different track from the port's container-and-EXIM economy or the IT-SEZ corridor, and one with no equivalent on Mumbai's or Thane's pages.",
  },
];

